@using LineOps.Web.Components.Desk
@using LineOps.Web.Windowing
@inherits PanelBase
@inject DeskToasts Toasts

@*
    The parts bin: every reusable control the desk owns, in one window you can open
    beside the thing you are building.

    It exists because the alternative is reading CSS to find out what already exists,
    which is how a second button gets invented. Being a window rather than a doc page
    is the point — it renders in the same column width, on the same surface, under the
    same theme as the panel it is being copied into, so "does this fit?" is answerable
    by looking rather than guessing.
*@

<Note Style="margin:12px 0 14px">
    Everything here is live. Hover a key to see it lift, press it to feel the throw, and
    tab through the row to check that the keyboard gets the same signal the pointer does.
</Note>

<h3 class="panel-h">Keys — tone</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    Gloss is the desk's affordance channel: a surface that catches the light can be
    clicked. Tone then states the consequence, using the same dial as everything else.
</p>

<div class="row" style="margin-bottom:6px">
    <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Neutral">Neutral</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Action" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Check">Action</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Go" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.PlayArrow">Go</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Caution" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Warning">Caution</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Stop" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.DeleteOutline">Stop</DeskButton>
</div>

<p class="empty" style="padding:2px 0 0">
    Name the outcome, not the colour — <span class="mono">Tone.Stop</span>, never "red".
    One <span class="mono">Action</span> per group; if two keys both look like the intent,
    neither is.
</p>

<h3 class="panel-h">Keys — state</h3>

<div class="row" style="margin-bottom:6px">
    <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Go" Busy="@_busy" OnClick="RunAsync">
        @(_busy ? "Ingesting" : "Press to run")
    </DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Action" Disabled="true">Disabled</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Quiet="true">Quiet</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Refresh" Title="Reload" />
</div>

<p class="empty" style="padding:2px 0 0">
    A busy key keeps its gloss and borrows the pulse strip's sweep — it is occupied, not
    inert. A disabled key loses the sheen outright, because under the rule above an unlit
    surface is one you cannot press. Quiet is a link wearing a hit area: it never lifts,
    so it cannot compete with the key beside it that commits.
</p>

<h3 class="panel-h">Keys — size</h3>

<div class="row">
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Tone="DeskTone.Neutral">Small</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Medium" Tone="DeskTone.Neutral">Medium</DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Large" Tone="DeskTone.Action">Large</DeskButton>
</div>

<h3 class="panel-h">Gate switch</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    One visible choice among a few — a market, a lookback, a mode. The cap is a key
    that never leaves its channel: it slides to the position you press, and arrows
    move it from the keyboard. Use it for 2–5 fixed positions; past that, a field
    select is the honest control.
</p>

<div class="row" style="margin-bottom:6px">
    <DeskSwitch Options="_gateMarkets" Label="Demo market" @bind-Value="_gateMarket" />
    <DeskSwitch Options="GateOptions.LookbackDays" Label="Demo lookback" Mono="true"
                @bind-Value="_gateDays" />
    <DeskSwitch Options="GateOptions.LookbackDays" Label="Demo lookback, small" Mono="true"
                Size="DeskKeySize.Small" @bind-Value="_gateDays" />
</div>

<p class="empty" style="padding:2px 0 0">
    <span class="mono">GateOptions.LookbackDays</span> is shared — a lookback window
    reads "14d / 30d / 90d" in every window, not two spellings of the same question.
</p>

<h3 class="panel-h">Tab bar</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    The same marble, doing the other job. A gate changes what one reading is made of;
    tabs change which reading you are looking at, so they are flat — nothing here is a
    key you press down. A section with nothing behind it is never offered, and one
    section on its own renders with no bar at all.
</p>

<DeskTabs TValue="string" Tabs="_demoTabs" Label="Demo sections" @bind-Value="_demoTab">
    <Note Style="margin:0 0 10px">
        Showing <span class="mono">@context</span>. The third tab is marked unavailable,
        so it is not on the bar — flip it to see the marble re-measure itself.
    </Note>
</DeskTabs>

<div class="row">
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Quiet="true" OnClick="() => _thirdTab = !_thirdTab">
        @(_thirdTab ? "Take the third section away" : "Give the third section content")
    </DeskButton>
</div>

<h3 class="panel-h">Fields</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    The key's inverse, cut into the panel instead of standing off it. Same height, so a
    row of mixed controls still lines up.
</p>

<div class="row">
    <input class="field" placeholder="Book" style="max-width:150px" @bind="_text" />
    <input class="field num" type="number" placeholder="Price" style="max-width:100px" @bind="_number" />
    <select class="field" @bind="_choice">
        <option value="spread">Spread</option>
        <option value="total">Total</option>
        <option value="moneyline">Moneyline</option>
    </select>
    <input class="field" placeholder="Disabled" disabled style="max-width:120px" />
</div>

<h3 class="panel-h">State words</h3>

<div class="row">
    <Tag>Neutral</Tag>
    <Tag Tone="DeskTone.Go">Healthy</Tag>
    <Tag Tone="DeskTone.Caution">Degraded</Tag>
    <Tag Tone="DeskTone.Stop">Breached</Tag>
    <Tag Tone="DeskTone.Action">Selected</Tag>
</div>

<h3 class="panel-h">Metrics</h3>

<MetricRow>
    <Metric Label="Uptime" Value="98.4%" Note="30-day rolling" Tone="DeskTone.Go" Numeric="true" />
    <Metric Label="Credits left" Value="412" Tone="DeskTone.Caution" Numeric="true" />
    <Metric Label="CLV" Value="-2.1%" Tone="DeskTone.Stop" Numeric="true" />
    <Metric Label="Sources" Value="6" Tone="DeskTone.Action" Numeric="true" />
</MetricRow>

<h3 class="panel-h">Pulse</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    The desk's other loud element, and the one a key's busy sweep borrows from. Set it
    from a panel with <span class="mono">Report(PulseState.Active)</span>.
</p>

<div class="stack" style="gap:6px">
    @foreach (var (state, label) in _pulses)
    {
        <div class="row" style="gap:10px">
            <div class="pulse @state" style="width:120px; border-radius:1px"></div>
            <span class="dim" style="font-size:11.5px">@label</span>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@* ---------------------------------------------------------------- Note ----- *@

<h3 class="panel-h">Notices</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    <span class="mono">&lt;Note&gt;</span> is the ambient tier of feedback: it sits beside the
    thing it describes and stays as long as the thing is true. No dismiss control, no timer —
    if it needs acknowledging it is a dialog, and if it should vanish it is a toast.
</p>

<div class="stack">
    <Note Style="margin:0">
        A note carries context the operator needs but did not ask for.
    </Note>
    <Note Tone="DeskTone.Caution" Style="margin:0">
        A warning carries a cost. State what it is, and what to do instead.
    </Note>
</div>

<p class="empty" style="padding:9px 0 0">
    Only <span class="mono">Tone.Caution</span> changes the skin, because a cost is the one
    thing an operator has to act on. The rule from ADR 0008 still holds inside the box: the
    flag rule never carries the message on its own — the sentence says what the cost is, and
    the hue only makes it findable.
</p>

<div class="row" style="gap:8px; margin-top:9px">
    <span class="dim" style="font-size:11.5px">At word scale, inside a row or cell:</span>
    <span class="mono" style="font-size:12px">
        <Note Inline Tone="DeskTone.Caution">62%</Note>
    </span>
</div>

@* ======================================================================
   Foundation — spacing ramp, type styles, and the two layout primitives.
   Appended section; keep new sections below this one.
   ====================================================================== *@

<h3 class="panel-h">Spacing</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    One 4px ramp. Pass a step to <span class="mono">DeskRow</span> or
    <span class="mono">DeskStack</span> rather than writing a pixel gap inline.
</p>

<DeskStack Gap="DeskSpace.Space1">
    @foreach (var (step, name, px) in _spaces)
    {
        <DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space3">
            <span class="mono dim" style="width:64px; font-size:11px">@name</span>
            <div style="height:8px; border-radius:1px; background:var(--iris); width:@px"></div>
            <span class="dim" style="font-size:11px">@px</span>
            <span class="dim" style="font-size:11px">@_spaceUse[step]</span>
        </DeskRow>
    }
</DeskStack>

<h3 class="panel-h">Type styles</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    Named for the job, not the size — <span class="mono">--text-body</span> keeps
    meaning the right thing when 13px stops being 13px.
</p>

<DeskStack Gap="DeskSpace.Space2">
    @foreach (var (token, size, leading, use) in _typeStyles)
    {
        <DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space3" Align="DeskAlign.Baseline">
            <span class="mono dim" style="width:104px; font-size:11px">@token</span>
            <span style="font-size:var(@token); line-height:var(@leading)">Line movement held</span>
            <span class="dim" style="font-size:11px">@size / @use</span>
        </DeskRow>
    }
</DeskStack>

<h3 class="panel-h">Row and stack</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    <span class="mono">DeskRow</span> is <span class="mono">.row</span> with its gap,
    alignment and justification named; <span class="mono">Justify="End"</span> is the
    old <span class="mono">.row--end</span>. <span class="mono">DeskStack</span> is the
    vertical one. Both leave the 8px default alone when Gap is unset.
</p>

<DeskStack Gap="DeskSpace.Space3">
    <DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space2">
        <span class="dim" style="font-size:11px">Row, Space2 — the default gap.</span>
        <span class="tag">one</span>
        <span class="tag">two</span>
    </DeskRow>

    <DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space4">
        <span class="dim" style="font-size:11px">Row, Space4.</span>
        <span class="tag">one</span>
        <span class="tag">two</span>
    </DeskRow>

    <DeskRow Justify="DeskJustify.End" Gap="DeskSpace.Space2">
        <DeskButton Quiet="true">Cancel</DeskButton>
        <DeskButton Tone="DeskTone.Action">Apply</DeskButton>
    </DeskRow>

    <DeskRow Justify="DeskJustify.Between">
        <span class="dim" style="font-size:11px">Justify=Between — label left, control right.</span>
        <span class="tag">right</span>
    </DeskRow>
</DeskStack>

@code {
    // Token name, rendered width, and what the rung is for.
    private static readonly (string Step, string Name, string Px)[] _spaces =
    [
        ("1", "--space-1", "4px"),
        ("2", "--space-2", "8px"),
        ("3", "--space-3", "12px"),
        ("4", "--space-4", "16px"),
        ("6", "--space-6", "24px"),
        ("8", "--space-8", "32px"),
        ("12", "--space-12", "48px"),
        ("16", "--space-16", "64px")
    ];

    private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> _spaceUse = new()
    {
        ["1"] = "a value and its unit",
        ["2"] = "the desk's default gap",
        ["3"] = "separate controls",
        ["4"] = "groups in a section",
        ["6"] = "sections of a panel",
        ["8"] = "read at different times",
        ["12"] = "page-level separation",
        ["16"] = "any more wants a divider"
    };

    private static readonly (string Token, string Size, string Leading, string Use)[] _typeStyles =
    [
        ("--text-title", "10.5px", "--leading-title", "panel headings"),
        ("--text-caption", "11px", "--leading-caption", "labels and units"),
        ("--text-body", "13px", "--leading-body", "default reading size"),
        ("--text-callout", "14px", "--leading-callout", "read this first")
    ];
@* ==== BEGIN: Loading vocabulary ============================================
   Skeletons and the progress bar. Appended as its own section; nothing above
   this marker belongs to it.
   ========================================================================= *@

<h3 class="panel-h">Skeletons</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    A placeholder holds the shape the real content will take, so the layout does not jump
    when it lands. Match the final shape — lines for prose, a block for a chart or a tile —
    and give it the width the real thing has. Matte, never glossy: there is nothing here to
    press. Reduced motion keeps the block and drops the shimmer.
</p>

<div class="stack" style="gap:12px; margin-bottom:6px">
    <div>
        <div class="dim" style="font-size:11px; margin-bottom:5px">One line, 40% wide</div>
        <DeskSkeleton Width="40%" />
    </div>
    <div>
        <div class="dim" style="font-size:11px; margin-bottom:5px">Three lines — the last runs short, the way prose does</div>
        <DeskSkeleton Lines="3" />
    </div>
    <div>
        <div class="dim" style="font-size:11px; margin-bottom:5px">A block, standing in for a chart</div>
        <DeskSkeleton Shape="DeskSkeletonShape.Block" Height="64px" />
    </div>
</div>

<h3 class="panel-h">Skeletons — grid rows</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    <span class="mono">DeskGrid</span> takes <span class="mono">Loading</span>: with no rows
    yet it stands aside and placeholder rows hold its rhythm. A grid that already has rows
    keeps them and reports the refresh through the pulse strip instead — replacing read
    numbers with grey bars loses information the operator was using.
</p>

<div class="row" style="margin-bottom:8px">
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" OnClick="@(() => _gridLoading = !_gridLoading)">
        @(_gridLoading ? "Show rows" : "Show placeholders")
    </DeskButton>
</div>

<DeskGrid T="PartRow" Items="@(_gridLoading ? Array.Empty<PartRow>() : _rows)" Loading="@_gridLoading"
          LoadingLabel="Fetching book prices" SkeletonRows="4" Sortable="false">
    <PropertyColumn T="PartRow" TProperty="string" Property="x => x.Book" Title="Book" />
    <PropertyColumn T="PartRow" TProperty="string" Property="x => x.Market" Title="Market" />
    <PropertyColumn T="PartRow" TProperty="string" Property="x => x.Price" Title="Price" />
</DeskGrid>

<h3 class="panel-h">Progress</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    Prefer determinate whenever the denominator is knowable — if you are walking six books,
    you know it. The label must name the action, never the wait: "Loading…" describes the
    operator's experience, which they already have, rather than the system's work, which
    they do not. Mode is a parameter, so it is chosen once and held; a bar that changes
    style mid-flight reads as a restart.
</p>

<div class="stack" style="gap:14px">
    <DeskProgress Label="Fetching odds from 6 books" Value="@_progress" Max="100" />
    <DeskProgress Indeterminate="true" Label="Reconciling open wagers" />
    <DeskProgress Indeterminate="true" />
</div>

<div class="row" style="margin-top:10px">
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Tone="DeskTone.Go" Busy="@_stepping" OnClick="StepAsync">
        Advance the bar
    </DeskButton>
</div>

@* ==== END: Loading vocabulary ============================================ *@

@code {
    private sealed record PartRow(string Book, string Market, string Price);

    private static readonly PartRow[] _rows =
    [
        new("Pinnacle", "Spread", "-110"),
        new("Circa", "Total", "-105"),
        new("DraftKings", "Moneyline", "+134"),
        new("FanDuel", "Spread", "-112")
    ];

    private bool _gridLoading = true;
    private double _progress = 34;
    private bool _stepping;

    // Deliberately fake, for the same reason the busy key above is: a determinate bar is
    // only legible while it is moving.
    private async Task StepAsync()
    {
        _stepping = true;

        try
        {
            for (var i = 0; i < 6; i++)
            {
                await Task.Delay(220);
                _progress = _progress >= 100 ? 0 : Math.Min(100, _progress + 11);
                StateHasChanged();
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            // Without the finally the key would stay lit for good on a throw, which is the
            // one thing a busy key must never do.
            _stepping = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    private static readonly (string Class, string Label)[] _pulses =
    [
        ("pulse--healthy", "Healthy — the source answered, and the numbers moved."),
        ("pulse--active", "Active — work is genuinely in flight."),
        ("pulse--warn", "Warn — degraded, or under budget pressure."),
        ("pulse--critical", "Critical — the one state you must not miss."),
        ("pulse--idle", "Idle — nothing has run yet.")
    ];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DeskSwitchOption<string>> _gateMarkets =
    [
        new("moneyline", "Moneyline"),
        new("spread", "Spread"),
        new("total", "Total")
    ];

    private string _gateMarket = "moneyline";
    private int _gateDays = 30;

    private string _demoTab = "Lines";
    private bool _thirdTab;

    private IReadOnlyList<DeskTab<string>> _demoTabs =>
    [
        new("Lines", "Lines"),
        new("Team data", "Team data"),
        new("Box score", "Box score", _thirdTab)
    ];

    private bool _busy;
    private string? _text;
    private decimal? _number;
    private string _choice = "spread";

    protected override void OnInitialized() => Report(PulseState.Idle, "reference");

    // Deliberately fake. A styleguide's job is to let you look at a state, and busy is a
    // state you otherwise have to catch mid-request to see.
    private async Task RunAsync()
    {
        _busy = true;
        Report(PulseState.Active, "demonstrating");

        await Task.Delay(1400);

        _busy = false;
        Report(PulseState.Healthy, "reference");
    }
}

@* ======================================================================== DeskChart *@

<h3 class="panel-h">Charts</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    <span class="mono">DeskChart</span> is the seam over MudBlazor's chart. A caller states
    the data, what kind of number it is, and one sentence a screen reader can use — never a
    colour. The family decides the series palette once, so the same kind of number reads the
    same in every window.
</p>

<DeskChart Series="@_movement" Labels="@_chartLabels" Height="180px"
           Family="DeskChartFamily.Movement"
           Headline="Movement — prices over time"
           Takeaway="Hue is identity here, not state: iris, steam, flag, drift, then haze."
           Description="Demonstration line chart with three series in the movement palette." />

<DeskChart Series="@_ledger" Labels="@_chartLabels" Height="160px"
           Family="DeskChartFamily.Ledger"
           ShowLegend="false"
           Headline="Ledger — money you kept"
           Takeaway="Opens on steam, because the desk already spells won in steam."
           Description="Demonstration line chart of a single cumulative units series." />

<DeskChart Series="@_health" Labels="@_chartLabels" Kind="DeskChartKind.Bar" Height="160px"
           Family="DeskChartFamily.Health"
           Headline="Health — how a source is behaving"
           Takeaway="Ordered like the pulse strip: healthy, warn, breached."
           Description="Demonstration bar chart with three series in the health palette." />

@code {
    // Deliberately fake, and shaped rather than random: a styleguide's job is to let you
    // compare palettes side by side, which needs the same X axis under each one.
    private static readonly string[] _chartLabels = ["Mon", "", "Wed", "", "Fri", ""];

    private static readonly List<ChartSeries<double>> _movement =
    [
        new() { Name = "Home · Pinnacle", Data = new double[] { -110, -115, -108, -120, -125, -118 } },
        new() { Name = "Home · Circa", Data = new double[] { -112, -110, -105, -118, -122, -120 } },
        new() { Name = "Away · Pinnacle", Data = new double[] { -102, -105, -112, -100, -96, -101 } }
    ];

    private static readonly List<ChartSeries<double>> _ledger =
    [
        new() { Name = "Cumulative", Data = new double[] { 0, 1.4, 0.6, 2.8, 2.1, 3.9 } }
    ];

    private static readonly List<ChartSeries<double>> _health =
    [
        new() { Name = "Healthy", Data = new double[] { 22, 24, 19, 26, 25, 27 } },
        new() { Name = "Warn", Data = new double[] { 3, 2, 6, 1, 2, 1 } },
        new() { Name = "Breached", Data = new double[] { 0, 1, 3, 0, 1, 0 } }
    ];
}
@* ============================================================== toasts ==== *@

@*
    The transient tier, demonstrated. It is last in the bin because it is the only part
    here that does not render in place — a toast lands in the corner of the desk rather
    than in this column, so the point of the section is the press, not the layout.
*@

<h3 class="panel-h">Toasts</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    A toast reports that something finished while you were looking somewhere else:
    non-critical, auto-dismissing, and recoverable from the panel that raised it. Anything
    needing a decision stays inline instead, because a notice that has dismissed itself
    cannot be acted on. Raise one with <span class="mono">Toasts.Success(...)</span> —
    panels take <span class="mono">DeskToasts</span>, never <span class="mono">ISnackbar</span>.
</p>

<div class="row" style="gap:8px; flex-wrap:wrap">
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Tone="DeskTone.Go"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Success("Evaluated — every source inside its SLO."))">
        Success
    </DeskButton>

    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Tone="DeskTone.Action"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Info("Layout saved to this browser."))">
        Info
    </DeskButton>

    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Tone="DeskTone.Caution"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Warn("Pull finished — 12 pulls left in today's allowance."))">
        Warn
    </DeskButton>

    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Tone="DeskTone.Stop"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Fail("Scheduled pull failed — the provider timed out."))">
        Fail
    </DeskButton>

    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Quiet="true"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Show("Nothing to report."))">
        Neutral
    </DeskButton>
</div>
@* ===== Desk/Tag demo — appended section, do not interleave ===================== *@

<h3 class="panel-h">Tag</h3>

<p class="dim" style="font-size:12px;max-width:56ch">
    The state word, as a component. A call site names the consequence and the modifier
    follows: Go for a healthy outcome, Caution for a cost worth knowing, Stop for a
    breach, Action for the row you picked, Neutral for a label that carries no state at
    all. The tag always says its word — hue is the second channel here, never the only one.
</p>

<div class="row">
    @foreach (var (tone, word) in TagDemoTones)
    {
        <Tag Tone="@tone">@word</Tag>
    }
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly (DeskTone Tone, string Word)[] TagDemoTones =
    [
        (DeskTone.Neutral, "Neutral"),
        (DeskTone.Action, "Action"),
        (DeskTone.Go, "Go"),
        (DeskTone.Stop, "Stop"),
        (DeskTone.Caution, "Caution")
    ];
}

@* ===== end Desk/Tag demo ====================================================== *@
@* ------------------------------------------------------------ EmptyState --- *@

<h3 class="panel-h">Empty states</h3>
<p class="empty" style="padding:0 0 9px">
    Nothing to show is still something to say. <span class="mono">Kind</span> picks which
    sentence the panel is writing — and the reader's next move follows from it, so the
    wrong kind sends them to check the feed when the filter was the problem.
</p>

<div class="stack" style="gap:2px">
    <EmptyState>
        Default — the bare line, unchanged. Loading notes and neutral asides live here.
    </EmptyState>

    <EmptyState Kind="EmptyStateKind.New">
        <ChildContent>
            New — nothing exists yet, so the message is about what would. The action is the
            first step, never a menu of them.
        </ChildContent>
        <Action>
            <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Tone="DeskTone.Action"
                        Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Add">Log the first entry</DeskButton>
        </Action>
    </EmptyState>

    <EmptyState Kind="EmptyStateKind.Filtered">
        <ChildContent>
            Filtered — rows exist; this window excluded them. Name the control that did it,
            so the reader looks at the filter instead of at the feed.
        </ChildContent>
        <Action>
            <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Quiet="true"
                        Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.FilterAltOff">Clear the filter</DeskButton>
        </Action>
    </EmptyState>

    <EmptyState Kind="EmptyStateKind.Error">
        <ChildContent>
            Error — the load failed, which a grey sentence alone would not distinguish from
            "nothing here yet". It takes the rule in <span class="mono">--flag</span> and the
            inset that comes with it: hue plus shape, never hue on its own.
        </ChildContent>
        <Action>
            <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Refresh">Retry</DeskButton>
        </Action>
    </EmptyState>
</div>
